WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.472
Picture a small company that records card payments for cafés and shops.

2
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:12.714
Every night, a program saves the day's payments as files in one shared folder.

3
00:00:12.714 --> 00:00:14.731
By now, it holds a whole year.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.961
A colleague in finance asks how much the shops took yesterday.

5
00:00:18.961 --> 00:00:21.581
The answer arrives twenty minutes later.

6
00:00:21.581 --> 00:00:23.219
Why so slow, for one day?

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.837
Because the program read the whole year to find one day.

8
00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:31.072
With one folder for the whole table, every question reads every file.

9
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:34.222
And the table grows by a day, every day.

10
00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:40.944
After three years, a question about yesterday reads 1,095 days of data, to use one of them.

11
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.252
A library with every book on one long shelf has the same problem.

12
00:00:45.252 --> 00:00:49.085
Ask for one year's books, and somebody walks the whole shelf.

13
00:00:49.085 --> 00:00:50.531
Usually the new person.

14
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.071
The library's old fix: one shelf per year, with the year on a label.

15
00:00:55.071 --> 00:00:58.735
A question about one year never visits the other shelves.

16
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:01.070
Let's do the same with the table.

17
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:06.425
Split the folder into smaller folders, one per day, and name each one after its day.

18
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:08.069
The shelf is a folder.

19
00:01:08.069 --> 00:01:10.092
The label is the folder's name.

20
00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:12.115
And the books are the payments.

21
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:18.308
Each of these folders is called a partition, and dt, short for date, is the partition column.

22
00:01:18.308 --> 00:01:25.284
The name holds the column and its value together, so any program can rebuild the column from the path alone.

23
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.809
In fact, the value lives only there.

24
00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:38.088
Spark, the engine this course uses to run jobs over many machines, takes the dt column out of the rows when it writes, and puts it in the folder name instead.

25
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:42.315
When a program reads the table, it turns the name back into a column.

26
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:43.874
Now ask for one day.

27
00:01:43.874 --> 00:01:46.154
Where dt is the third of November.

28
00:01:46.154 --> 00:01:53.263
The program reads the labels first, and skips every folder that can't match, before opening a single file.

29
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:55.585
That's called partition pruning.

30
00:01:55.585 --> 00:02:03.318
In the lesson's week of payments, DuckDB, a small database that runs inside a program, opens one file out of eight.

31
00:02:03.318 --> 00:02:06.680
Over three years, one day out of about a thousand.

32
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:08.430
Now a different filter.

33
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:14.399
Every payment inside the files also has its exact time, in a column called created at.

34
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.036
Ask for the payments whose created at is on the third of November.

35
00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:20.245
The very same day.

36
00:02:20.245 --> 00:02:22.127
How many files does it open?

37
00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:23.203
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.132
All eight.

39
00:02:27.132 --> 00:02:32.472
The answer is right, nothing fails, and nobody notices the extra reading.

40
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.199
The program only reads labels, and the labels say dt.

41
00:02:36.199 --> 00:02:39.038
It can't know that created at and dt agree.

42
00:02:39.038 --> 00:02:40.293
Only you know that.

43
00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:44.716
The program would need to be a mind reader, and it's a file reader.

44
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.851
The plan, the program's own description of how it will run a query, tells you.

45
00:02:49.851 --> 00:02:55.563
In DuckDB, a line like scanning files, one of eight, means it skipped files by their path.

46
00:02:55.563 --> 00:02:57.912
No such line means it reads them all.

47
00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:02.831
In Spark, an empty list of partition filters means nothing was skipped.

48
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:09.760
So, the rule: a filter on the partition column skips folders, and any other filter reads them all.

49
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.132
Put dt in the query.

50
00:03:11.132 --> 00:03:14.701
Keep the created at filter too, for the exact hours.

51
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.883
And pick a partition column that questions filter on, with few different values.

52
00:03:19.883 --> 00:03:23.745
A day is the classic: three years is about a thousand folders.

53
00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:32.472
Split by shop as well, with 500,000 shops, and you get 500 million folders, most holding one tiny file.

54
00:03:32.472 --> 00:03:33.793
Strike that one.

55
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.209
The moral: the program skips by the label, never by what's inside.

56
00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:42.099
Name the folders by what you ask about, and ask by that name.

57
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:43.714
One quick check.

58
00:03:43.714 --> 00:03:46.581
Thirty folders, one per day of November.

59
00:03:46.581 --> 00:03:49.735
You ask for dt on or after the twenty ninth.

60
00:03:49.735 --> 00:03:51.885
How many folders does it open?

61
00:03:51.885 --> 00:03:53.032
Pause and think.

62
00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:56.559
Two.

63
00:03:56.559 --> 00:03:59.113
The twenty ninth and the thirtieth.

64
00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:02.836
Ask by created at instead, and it opens all thirty.
